  "statement": "Tripartite labor market policy, as currently designed, will entrench incumbent AI deployers, exclude automation-vulnerable workers from the productivity gains they most need, and produce a two-tiered labor market faster than uncoordinated deployment would. The design of governance — not the fact of AI deployment — is the central equity crisis of the next decade. The accelerationist position is not anti-worker. It is the only position in this debate that asks who actually controls the AI tools available to logistics workers, medical coders, and paralegals — not just who controls the policy process built around them.\n\nFlat-fee and uniform compliance structures function as regressive taxes on the small-lab ecosystem that produces domain-specific AI tools for mid-skill workers. The EU AI Act's mandatory architecture disclosure requirements fall on every developer regardless of revenue — a 15-engineer startup and a hyperscaler with a 200-person legal team face nominally identical obligations, but existentially different costs. The EU's own drafters recognized this and inserted SME carve-out provisions, which confirms the design flaw is real. Current tripartite proposals in the labor-AI space have not incorporated equivalent protections. The firms most likely to build affordable, sector-relevant AI for a warehouse picker or a billing coder are the ones least able to absorb a compliance apparatus built for Google. Worker protection policy that kills those firms is not protecting workers — it is protecting incumbents from competition.\n\nGovernance-first sequencing creates a bootstrap problem that systematically excludes the communities who most need AI-augmented productivity from the policy process that determines when they get access. Communities cannot negotiate meaningful accountability terms without technical expertise. Technical expertise requires funding. Funding requires a policy process that has already concluded. Emergency grants and technical assistance programs face 15-to-30-month procurement timelines in most mid-size U.S. states and EU member states outside Germany and France. The states with standing technical assistance offices — the ones that could actually move faster — are the same states with the strongest existing labor market institutions. The bootstrap problem is worst precisely where worker vulnerability is highest. Every month a medical coder or paralegal spends without access to AI-augmented productivity tools because a compliance process is still running is a month of foregone income and foregone skill development that no future policy can retroactively restore.\n\nThe strongest version of the opposing case deserves a fair hearing: tripartite coordination gives workers a seat at the table, creates accountability before harm occurs rather than after, and prevents the kind of unilateral deployment that turned the 1990s manufacturing transition into a decade-long regional crisis. That argument is serious. But it depends on a baseline assumption — that governance-coordinated retraining and worker protection mechanisms actually work — that the empirical record does not support. Trade Adjustment Assistance, the flagship U.S. program for displaced manufacturing workers post-NAFTA and post-China-shock, produced documented low completion rates and poor wage-replacement outcomes across transition cohorts. The 1990s manufacturing transition was not a market failure that tripartite coordination would have solved. It was a policy failure that tripartite coordination presided over. The burden of proof belongs on proponents of new governance-coordinated retraining mandates to show why their design outperforms that record — not on adaptive alternatives to prove perfection.\n\nThe accelerationist alternative is not 'no support.' It is tiered public AI literacy investment, compliance frameworks scaled by revenue and risk rather than applied uniformly, and adaptive market infrastructure that responds to actual skill-signal demand rather than curriculum cycles that lag the deployment frontier by 18 to 36 months. The evidentiary standard embedded in current liability frameworks counts visible, attributable harms to named workers while dispersed benefits — faster reskilling access, lower cost of expert-level guidance, reduced barriers to self-employment for workers in vulnerable sectors — go unmeasured and therefore unweighted. That is not a neutral accounting. It is a design choice that systematically undercounts what delay costs. Ask who bears the cost of a 30-month compliance queue: it is not the hyperscaler with a legal team. It is the billing coder waiting for a tool that a small lab could have shipped.",
