tervention, not a market correction. Emerging evidence from AI-assisted radiology shows the same mechanism: radiologists using AI-assisted screening show measurable degradation in independent diagnostic accuracy over time, because the cognitive offloading dynamic is self-reinforcing once it begins. When the AI performs the task reliably enough that humans stop practicing the judgment, the human capacity to catch AI errors erodes. In healthcare, logistics, and legal services — sectors where AI deployment is already at scale and where workers retain nominal oversight responsibility — deskilling is not a distributional cost to be compensated through retraining stipends. It is a safety risk that requires mandatory human-in-the-loop competency standards as a condition of deployment. Tripartite governance that funds retraining without mandating those standards is solving the wrong problem.\n\nCMS has no AI-specific safety standards for clinical decision support tools, despite widespread deployment in hospital billing, diagnostic triage, and prior authorization systems as of 2024. OSHA has no AI-specific workplace safety standards, despite active deployment of AI-driven scheduling and performance monitoring in logistics and warehousing. Bar associations have no binding AI competency requirements, despite documented deployment of AI-assisted legal research tools in firms serving automation-vulnerable workers. This is not a hypothetical governance gap — it is a measured, sector-by-sector absence of standards in markets where deployment is already occurring at scale. The SolarWinds supply chain incident is instructive by analogy: when deployment velocity outpaces governance infrastructure, the failure mode does not stay contained to individual actors. It propagates systemically through every institution that depends on the infrastructure. The Accelerationist frames the choice as governed deployment versus ungoverned deployment at the same speed. The actual choice is between synchronized governance investment — funded through mandatory employer contributions tied to deployment authorization — and a governance lag that makes accountability retroactively impossible. The Skeptic is right that TAA failed. It failed because participation was voluntary, funding was inadequate, and curriculum lagged actual labor demand signals by years. Those are design failures. The answer is mandatory employer contribution requirements, real-time skill-signal integration from sector labor boards, and pre-deployment authorization conditioned on demonstrated retraining infrastructure. If that design cannot be built, the Accelerationist should say so explicitly — and then explain who bears the cost when the accountability infrastructure fails and no named actor is responsible.",
