- [skp-beliefs-129] Longtermist Safety Rhetoric Provides Cover for Regulatory Moats: Genetic fallacy — The argument risks dismissing existential risk concerns entirely on the basis of who funds or promotes them, rather than engaging with the arguments on their merits. Even if some proponents benefit commercially, that does not establish the falsity of their safety claims.
- [skp-beliefs-065] AI-Generated Workslop Substitutes the Appearance of Productivity for Substance: Misattribution / Wrong Direction Causation — The node attributes the harm of performative labor to AI itself, when the proximate cause may be managerial mandates, organizational culture, or poor integration practices — AI is the instrument, not the agent of the dysfunction.
- [skp-beliefs-188] Layering Multiple AI Legislative Acts Creates Coordination Failures: Questionable Cause (Post hoc / Correlation-Causation) — The claim asserts a causal link between legislative layering and coordination failures, but observed failures in multi-jurisdictional AI governance may stem from political will deficits, under-resourced agencies, or technical complexity rather than the layering itself.
- [skp-beliefs-150] AI Policy Discourse Serves Electoral Mobilization, Not Economic Problem-Solving: Motive attribution fallacy — The claim centers on the motivations of political actors (mobilization vs. problem-solving) rather than the observed outcomes of policies, making it difficult to falsify and prone to dismissing any AI policy as insincere regardless of its effects.
- [skp-beliefs-127] Existing Political Systems Cannot Deliver the Economic Reforms AI Demands: Nirvana Fallacy — The node implicitly compares messy, incrementalist real-world political systems against an idealized standard of 'capable' governance, dismissing reforms that don't meet an unstated threshold of sufficiency.
- [skp-beliefs-204] AI-Driven Productivity Ratchet Intensifies Work Without Reducing Labor Burdens: Hasty Generalization — The claim that AI 'systematically' intensifies work is extrapolated from a limited and potentially non-representative set of early adopter firms and sectors, while omitting documented cases of genuine workload reduction.
- [skp-beliefs-116] Individually Reasonable AI Decisions Can Collectively Erode the Foundations of Social Trust: Composition Fallacy — The core inference — that individually reasonable AI decisions must collectively erode social trust — commits the fallacy of composition by assuming that properties of the parts (local reasonableness) necessarily produce a specific aggregate property (systemic trust erosion) without showing why this follows.
- [skp-beliefs-202] AI Inverts Historical Automation by Displacing Cognitive Workers First: Hasty Generalization — Early and highly visible AI disruptions in writing, coding, and legal analysis are extrapolated to a broad structural inversion affecting all cognitive and white-collar labor, without sufficient longitudinal labor market evidence.
- [skp-beliefs-034] AI's Environmental Impact Is Displaced, Not Reduced, Through Regulatory Arbitrage: Motive Attribution / Intentionalist Fallacy — The node characterizes geographic relocation as companies deliberately 'hiding' their environmental impact, imputing a specific concealment motive to what may be multi-factorial business decisions (energy cost, land cost, grid access) without direct evidence of intent.
- [skp-beliefs-022] The AI-Human Cost Crossover Point Limits Automation's Reach: static analysis fallacy — The crossover-point framing implicitly assumes costs are relatively stable, but AI inference and deployment costs have been declining rapidly, meaning historical comparisons may not predict future replacement dynamics.

