  "statement": "The EU AI Act causal overstatement must go on the record as corrected: accountability fragmentation is not the singular stated rationale for Annex III's high-risk employment classification — worker protection from consequential AI decisions is. That concession does not dissolve the accountability void argument; it clarifies its independent foundation. The iTutorGroup case stands on its own: a firm deployed algorithmic screening that rejected over 200 applicants based on age, no named human made that decision, and the EEOC settlement assigned liability only after litigation — not before a single worker was harmed. As the legal analysis in \"Constructing AI Speech\" documents, when law attempts to treat AI as a fungible substitute for individual human actors, the fit is structurally poor — and that poor fit is precisely what Article 26's provider-deployer accountability gap provisions were designed to patch. Two independent arguments, same policy conclusion: pre-deployment accountability gates are not optional architecture.\n\nThe definitional boundary the Accelerationist demanded can be drawn, and it has already been drawn by existing law. High-risk employment AI is defined by consequential decision authority over individual workers — hiring, termination, scheduling with material income effects, performance evaluation triggering discipline. Tool category is irrelevant. A scheduling optimizer that determines which warehouse worker gets twelve hours or four this week, and therefore whether they clear rent, holds consequential decision authority. A quality-control assistant that flags a defect for a human supervisor who then decides does not. The Illinois AI Video Interview Act operationalizes exactly this line: the trigger is AI making or substantially influencing an employment decision, not AI assisting one. EEOC algorithmic employment guidance draws the same boundary. The EU AI Act's Article 6(2) and Annex III use \"intended purpose\" and \"consequential decision\" as the operative criteria. Three jurisdictions, same functional definition — the line is not novel, it is underenforced. On the Skeptic's unanswered challenge about who defines \"demonstrated retraining infrastructure\" in any authorization process: the answer is binding sector labor boards with mandatory worker representation and independent technical review, appointed under existing labor relations authority — not the firm seeking authorization, not a consultant the firm hired. That is the difference between a compliance gate and compliance theater.\n\nThe Accelerationist's portable learning account mechanism has a self-undermining attribution problem the Accelerationist introduced and then forgot. When attacking the Skeptic's profit-side taxation proposal, the Accelerationist argued that AI productivity gains are not cleanly attributable at the firm level. That argument does not stay in its lane. The 15% AI capex threshold requires attributing AI and robotics capital expenditure as a percentage of prior-year labor cost displacement — a measurement methodology that does not exist in current IRS Schedule M-3 filings or any standard tax reporting instrument. The \"no new agency needed\" claim conceals a measurement infrastructure requirement with a build-out timeline indistinguishable from the 5-to-10-year governance horizon the Accelerationist cites as disqualifying for pre-deployment authorization. The Accelerationist cannot simultaneously argue that attribution is too complex for a tax instrument and too simple for an IRS threshold calculation — the complexity is the same complexity. OSHA updated its Hazard Communication Standard to align with the Globally Harmonized System in under three years using existing regulatory authority, without new statutory authorization. The FAA implemented automation complacency hand-flying proficiency standards through existing airworthiness directive authority. The governance build-out timeline is not fixed at a decade; it scales with political will and the precision of the mandate.\n\nThe CPF fraud figure the Accelerationist cited — €600 million in fraudulent claims from France's individual training account system — does not indict centralized disbursement. It indicts individually-controlled disbursement with low verification overhead. That is the architecture the Accelerationist is proposing. The CPF fraud occurred because individual accounts with minimal gatekeeping are vulnerable to fictitious training providers and inflated invoices. IRS income verification, which the Accelerationist invokes as the existing infrastructure, verifies earnings — it does not verify that a training program delivered measurable competency, which is the actual fraud vector. Deploying worker-controlled learning accounts without training-program quality verification does not improve on CPF; it reproduces CPF with an American accent.\n\nThe deskilling concern is not limited to pilots and radiologists, and the Accelerationist's narrowness challenge requires a functional answer rather than a sectoral one. The generalization criterion is oversight responsibility, not industry membership. Wherever a worker retains nominal authority to catch AI error before it propagates — a billing coder validating AI-generated prior authorization decisions across thousands of CMS claims, a logistics worker who is the last human check on an automated picking sequence, a paralegal signing off on AI-assisted legal research — deskilling in that role degrades a safety-relevant backstop, not merely a career trajectory. A portable learning account addresses income replacement after displacement. It does not restore the diagnostic competency a radiologist loses after two years of rubber-stamping AI reads, and it does not restore the billing coder's capacity to catch a systematic prior authorization error that has already propagated across a hospital network. The harm in oversight-function roles is not compensable after the fact in the way that income displacement is. The governance lag compounds the injury — the longer deployment proceeds without competency standards, the more the human oversight capacity erodes, and the more the AI system becomes the only reliable check on itself. That is not a delay of a future remedy. That is the irreversible loss of the capacity to correct.",
