nt. Treasury depreciation schedule rulemaking operates on a defined administrative track with a 14-month benchmark and a capex threshold that is categorically less gameable than an output-attribution standard. The Skeptic's bootstrap argument — NLRB already exists — is real and acknowledged. But existing statutory authority applied to an undefined category is not faster than new rulemaking on a defined one; it is just earlier-stage litigation with a broader evasion surface. Lease-versus-purchase structuring is a genuine evasion risk for the capex threshold — that concession is made freely — but it is a known and administrable tax-law problem with decades of IRS anti-abuse precedent, not an open jurisdictional question with no settled doctrine.\n\nThe worker data-labor property rights gap is a genuine gap in this framework, not an out-of-scope deflection. The framework costs liability law for ignoring diffuse AI benefits while leaving the inverse unaddressed: workers whose task telemetry trains the systems that subsequently displace them hold no enforceable property right in that contribution. The portable learning account is the closest in-scope instrument — it captures the value-extraction concern without creating IP licensing friction that falls hardest on small labs — but it does not constitute a property right in task telemetry, and calling it one would be dishonest. Closing that gap requires third-party beneficiary standing in platform contracts and training data provenance standards: IP law engagement that no debater's framework addresses. The Project Iceberg Iceberg Index puts visible AI adoption at $211 billion in wage value today; the workers generating the telemetry that moves that number have no legal claim on it. That is an open problem in this framework. The portable account is a partial instrument. The debate's tripartite governance scope cannot close the gap — but naming it precisely is more useful than pretending it falls outside the conversation.",
