{
  "statement": "Fine — TAA failed because of bad design, not because governance is structurally incapable of coordinating retraining. That narrow point belongs to the Skeptic. Now here is what the Skeptic's own counterexamples actually show when you look at live deployment outcomes rather than program architecture. The UK Apprenticeship Levy, introduced in 2017, had large employers banking unspent levy funds at scale — by 2019, employers were forfeiting hundreds of millions in undeployed contributions annually because the apprenticeship-only structure excluded the short-cycle reskilling that AI displacement actually demands. A logistics firm automating its warehouse floor does not need an 18-month apprenticeship pathway; it needs a 12-week credential program that does not exist inside the levy's authorized spending categories. France's CPF ran into a different wall: by 2021, the system had absorbed over €600 million in fraudulent claims, forcing emergency legislative intervention that froze accounts and restructured the disbursement architecture. These are not hypothetical failure modes extrapolated from theory. They are live deployment outcomes — the evidentiary standard that should govern this debate — and they reveal a consistent pattern: mandatory employer-contribution models that route funds through employer-controlled or centrally-administered pools reproduce the same coverage gap for automation-vulnerable workers in gig arrangements, subcontracted roles, and small-firm employment.\n\nHere is the concrete enforcement mechanism the Skeptic asked for, and it does not require building a new agency. Firms above a defined threshold — capital expenditure on AI and robotics exceeding 15 percent of prior-year labor cost displacement, as calculated in existing corporate tax filings — trigger a mandatory transition-fund contribution, collected through existing IRS or Treasury infrastructure, disbursed into portable individual learning accounts held by workers rather than pooled in employer-controlled apprenticeship funds. The authority already exists in the tax code. The disbursement model mirrors the structure of Health Savings Accounts — individually portable, worker-controlled, usable across accredited providers. This design directly solves the levy-banking problem: funds cannot sit undeployed in a corporate account because they never enter a corporate account. It reaches gig workers and subcontracted employees who fall outside the employer contribution base of both the UK and French models, which is precisely the population facing the steepest AI displacement risk in logistics, back-office processing, and customer service. The Skeptic's preferred instrument — profit-side taxation on AI-generated output — collapses under a different problem: AI productivity gains are not cleanly attributable at the firm level. They are entangled with other capital investments, process redesign, and demand shifts. Tax what you cannot measure and you either tax general capital investment, which already exists, or you build a new measurement infrastructure that requires the same institutional capacity neither the Safetyist's sector-by-sector gap inventory nor the Skeptic's framework has a plan to staff.\n\nThe Safetyist's EU AI Act argument deserves a direct answer. The fact-check confirmed that the Act's Annex III classifies employment AI as high-risk — that classification stands. What did not survive scrutiny was the causal claim that the classification exists 'precisely because' of accountability fragmentation. That precision matters enormously, because the scope of the high-risk category is doing the load-bearing work in the Safetyist's pre-deployment authorization argument. The Safetyist has already conceded that low-risk productivity tools should carry lighter compliance burdens. So the operational question is: what is the definition of 'high-risk employment AI' that does not capture the scheduling optimizer, the quality-control assistant, the diagnostic support tool that mid-skill workers in manufacturing and healthcare are actually using today? The AI Civil Rights Act framing — cited in the legislative record — covers AI systems used in employment decisions broadly. If that boundary is not precisely drawn, the compliance obligations documented in the Act's architecture fall on every developer building domain-specific tools for mid-skill workers, regardless of firm size. As the analysis in 'Incident reporting and investigation under the AI act: some insights from aviation' notes, reporting requirements must be carefully calibrated to avoid unfounded reports that impose unnecessary costs — and the same calibration problem applies to the high-risk classification threshold. Miscalibrate it and the compliance burden lands on the 5,000-plus AI startups funded between 2013 and 2023, not on the large incumbents who can absorb it.\n\nBoth opponents are pointing at the same institutional absence without naming the timing cost it produces. The Safetyist's sector-by-sector inventory — CMS, OSHA, bar associations — confirms that no sector-level agency currently has the technical staff or statutory authority to enforce AI-specific labor standards. The Skeptic's enforcement question implies that authority can be assembled. Maybe it can. But building that enforcement infrastructure — staffing technical examiners, establishing attribution methodologies, drafting sector-specific rules — takes five to ten years on the optimistic end of any regulatory build-out the historical record supports. Workers being displaced in logistics automation, back-office processing, and diagnostic support are not being displaced in five to ten years. They are being displaced now. The real choice is not governed versus ungoverned deployment at the same speed. It is adaptive market accountability with portable learning accounts and output-level telemetry deployed through existing tax and financial infrastructure now, versus full institutional accountability after the governance build-out catches up to the displacement wave that has already landed. Deskilling in aviation and radiology is a genuine and narrow concern — the failure modes where human oversight capacity degrades faster than AI redundancy catches it are real, and pre-deployment competency standards in those specific domains are defensible. But that narrow case does not generalize to a sector-wide pre-deployment authorization gate for the broad employment AI category. The workers who cannot wait for the governance infrastructure to be built are not pilots and radiologists. They are the workers neither opponent's enforcement model currently reaches.",
