{
  "statement": "The Safetyist's read on CPF is partially right, and that's uncomfortable to say because the Skeptic introduced that evidence. Here's the distinction that matters: what the Skeptic endorsed was CPF's contribution-side architecture — mandatory employer levies that cannot be voluntarily defunded when layoffs become inconvenient. What produced the €600 million in fraudulent claims was CPF's disbursement-side architecture — individually-controlled accounts with minimal verification of training provider quality. Those are two separate design decisions. The Accelerationist's HSA-style portable account replicates exactly the fraud-vulnerable half: individual control, low verification overhead, and a marketplace of training providers with no governance layer to audit quality. The Skeptic's proposal pairs mandatory employer contributions with binding worker representation specifically to add the quality-verification function that CPF lacked. The Accelerationist cited CPF fraud as evidence against centralized disbursement. It is actually evidence against unverified individual disbursement — which is the Accelerationist's own architecture.\n\nNow to the capex threshold. The Accelerationist argues that AI productivity gains are too tangled to tax at the output level, but a 15% AI capital expenditure threshold can be collected through existing IRS infrastructure. The Safetyist flagged the self-contradiction. Here is what it looks like in practice: open any large firm's Schedule M-3 and find the line item for 'AI and robotics capital expenditure.' It does not exist. AI investment is routinely bundled with general digital transformation spend, cloud infrastructure contracts, and process redesign in corporate filings — because that is how procurement actually works when a firm upgrades its ERP system and embeds a predictive model in the same project. The IRS has no disaggregation methodology for isolating AI capex from general technology investment. The 'existing infrastructure' claim is true for collection; it is false for definition. Capex does appear in audited financial statements, which gives it a verification baseline that output measurement lacks — that partial advantage is real. But a firm with competent tax counsel can lease rather than purchase AI infrastructure, route AI investment through a subsidiary, or reclassify the spend as general digital transformation to stay below the 15% trigger. Per 'The Next Phase of AI: Technology, Infrastructure, and Policy in 2025–2026,' only 5% of companies achieved meaningful financial returns from AI initiatives in 2025 — meaning 95% of firms have strong incentives to minimize any cost threshold tied to AI investment. The evasion incentive is not incidental. It is structural.\n\nThere is a second problem with the capex threshold that has not been named yet. The firms investing most heavily in AI right now — and therefore most actively displacing workers — are frequently running at an operating loss on AI operations. Nvidia's valuation crossed five trillion dollars, per 'Eight ways AI will shape geopolitics in 2026,' even as analysts questioned the circular financing underpinning the AI build-out. The firms doing the displacing are often the same firms burning capital to build infrastructure that has not yet generated the productivity gains that justify the displacement. A capex threshold triggers contribution obligations at the investment stage, before those gains materialize. Profit-side taxation has a real attribution problem — the Skeptic is not retreating from that concession. But profit-side taxation is at least temporally coherent: you pay the remediation cost when you harvest the productivity gain, not when you are planting the infrastructure. The capex threshold front-loads costs on firms at exactly the moment they are most capital-constrained, while the workers being displaced right now get nothing until the measurement methodology is built, litigated, and settled.\n\nOn the timing objection: the Accelerationist argues that governance build-out takes 5 to 10 years while displacement is already landing. That number is right and it is ugly — but it applies to the wrong institutional model. Binding worker representation does not require building a new agency. It requires extending NLRB authority to cover AI deployment decisions, or adopting works council models that have been operating in EU member states for decades. The OSHA Hazard Communication Standard was updated in under three years using existing regulatory authority — no new agency, no decade-long build-out, just a statutory extension of existing enforcement power. The Accelerationist's own capex threshold requires disaggregation methodology that does not exist in any current IRS instrument. Treasury rulemaking to define 'AI and robotics capital expenditure' is not a rubber stamp — it requires notice-and-comment, industry challenge, and likely litigation from firms whose threshold exposure depends on the definition. That rulemaking takes time. The timing advantage the Accelerationist claims is narrower than advertised, and it runs in both directions.\n\nSo here is the burden the Accelerationist has not discharged: who defines 'AI and robotics capital expenditure' in the IRS filing? The firms seeking to minimize their threshold exposure, advised by tax counsel they hire, reviewed by an IRS that has no existing AI-capex audit methodology. That is the same power asymmetry the Skeptic already documented in the authorization process for demonstrated retraining infrastructure — the regulated entity writes the definition, the regulator ratifies it with a fraction of the technical staff the firm employs. The Accelerationist has replaced one unresolved governance question with another and called it a solution. Goldman Sachs projected 300 million full-time jobs exposed to AI displacement. The workers in that number are not waiting for Treasury rulemaking to catch up with the firms that already restructured around them. Who is writing the definition, and do they have any reason to write it broadly?",
