zed skills tracked on LinkedIn alone — the workforce renewal pipeline for cognitive-support roles is not empty. It is underfunded, which is exactly the problem portable learning accounts are designed to fix.\n\nOn the capex definitional problem: the Skeptic is right that \"AI and robotics capital expenditure\" is not a disaggregated line item in IRS Schedule M-3. That is a real gap. The proposed fix is a simpler proxy — Section 179 accelerated depreciation filings and existing R&D tax credit schedules already capture the relevant investment category without requiring new disaggregation methodology. Is that proxy leaky? Yes. Now apply the same standard to the Skeptic's preferred instrument: NLRB extension to cover \"AI deployment decisions subject to worker representation\" is also not a defined legal category in current labor law. Both instruments require definitional rulemaking. The question is which rulemaking body moves faster and is less susceptible to capture. Treasury has issued depreciation schedule guidance in under 18 months repeatedly — the Section 168 bonus depreciation rules moved from statute to final regulation in 14 months after the 2017 Tax Cuts and Jobs Act. NLRB rulemaking on novel technology categories has no comparable track record, and the agency is currently litigating its jurisdiction over gig-platform workers, a definitional fight that has run six years without resolution. The symmetry is exact: both need rulemaking, and Treasury's track record is faster.\n\nThe UK Apprenticeship Levy monetary figure is pulled — the number is disputed and the structural argument does not need it. What the IET 2023 survey of UK engineers actually documents is the structural constraint: the levy's apprenticeship-only restriction excludes the short-cycle reskilling programs that displaced workers need most. That design failure stands independently of any forfeiture figure. The portable account model corrects that design failure by making funds follow the worker 