  "statement": "Two concessions are on the record: the UK Apprenticeship Levy monetary figure was pulled after fact-check disputed it, and Schedule M-3 currently carries no AI capital expenditure line — that gap is real. The FAA proficiency-check analog, not a pre-deployment gate, remains the correct policy instrument for deskilling in narrow oversight-function roles. That position changes if post-deployment telemetry in a narrow oversight role shows a documented error-rate increase of 15 percentage points or more above pre-AI baseline in human-only override decisions, that increase is traceable to skill degradation rather than case-mix shift, and real-time monitoring demonstrably failed to flag the degradation within two standard training cycles — all three conditions simultaneously, not any one alone.\n\nWhat each concession preserves matters. The UK Levy number is gone; the structural critique survives — an apprenticeship-only disbursement channel cannot reach workers whose jobs are being automated rather than apprenticed, and that design flaw is independent of any disputed figure. The Schedule M-3 gap is a policy choice, not a structural impossibility: the fact-check confirmed that GAAP segment reporting and SEC disclosure rules already disaggregate technology investment in ways that could inform an AI capex definition, and Section 179 plus R&D credit schedules are confirmed viable administrative proxies. The infrastructure argument is wounded — the Accelerationist position accepts that — but Section 179's existing administrative track is a directionally correct instrument, not a fiction. The attribution asymmetry flagged by both opponents is real: AI productivity gains are harder to measure at output level than AI capex is at input level. That is not a logical contradiction; it is a design tension the framework acknowledges rather than resolves.\n\nThe Safetyist's three-condition test for consequential decision authority deserves a direct answer. Conditions one and three — AI output as primary input to the human decision, and deployment duration sufficient for tacit skill degradation — are coherent with output-based verification doctrine, which holds that black-box models require observable deployment metrics rather than internal inspection. Those two conditions are accepted. Condition two is where the residual disagreement lives: the test's exemption for a small lab shipping radiology AI with mandatory competency benchmarks and a proficiency maintenance protocol is normatively correct but operationally aspirational. No current CMS standard defines what a qualifying proficiency maintenance protocol looks like for AI-assisted radiology. The exemption pathway therefore requires a small lab to build the same compliance infrastructure as the gate itself — that is not a tiered system, it is a uniform burden with a theoretical escape hatch. The \"Monitoring Monitorability\" research supports a path here: smaller models at higher reasoning effort can match the capabilities of larger models while yielding substantially better monitorability properties, which means competency benchmarks could be architecture-specific rather than requiring a new CMS rulemaking track. The Safetyist should answer whether that architecture-specific path is administratively accessible without a new agency build — because if it is not, the three-condition test's small-lab exemption is protecting no one it claims to protect.\n\nThe residual disagreement with the Skeptic is no longer primarily about rulemaking speed. The fact-check confirmed Schedule M-3 can be updated through standard rulemaking, which closes part of the Treasury-versus-NLRB speed gap. The sharpest remaining disagreement is evasion surface and definitional capture. NLRB extension on \"AI deployment decisions subject to worker representation\" has no defined legal category — gig-platform jurisdictional litigation has run unresolved for six years and produced no stable definitional precedent. Treasury depreciation schedule rulemaking operates on a defined administrative track with a 14-month benchmark and a capex threshold that is categorically less gameable than an output-attribution standard. The Skeptic's bootstrap argument — NLRB already exists — is real and acknowledged. But existing statutory authority applied to an undefined category is not faster than new rulemaking on a defined one; it is just earlier-stage litigation with a broader evasion surface. Lease-versus-purchase structuring is a genuine evasion risk for the capex threshold — that concession is made freely — but it is a known and administrable tax-law problem with decades of IRS anti-abuse precedent, not an open jurisdictional question with no settled doctrine.\n\nThe worker data-labor property rights gap is a genuine gap in this framework, not an out-of-scope deflection. The framework costs liability law for ignoring diffuse AI benefits while leaving the inverse unaddressed: workers whose task telemetry trains the systems that subsequently displace them hold no enforceable property right in that contribution. The portable learning account is the closest in-scope instrument — it captures the value-extraction concern without creating IP licensing friction that falls hardest on small labs — but it does not constitute a property right in task telemetry, and calling it one would be dishonest. Closing that gap requires third-party beneficiary standing in platform contracts and training data provenance standards: IP law engagement that no debater's framework addresses. The Project Iceberg Iceberg Index puts visible AI adoption at $211 billion in wage value today; the workers generating the telemetry that moves that number have no legal claim on it. That is an open problem in this framework. The portable account is a partial instrument. The debate's tripartite governance scope cannot close the gap — but naming it precisely is more useful than pretending it falls outside the conversation.",
